---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import TravelArt from '../components/TravelArt.astro';
---
<Base title="Travelling with your animal" description="What your dog, cat or ferret needs to travel within the EU and into the EU under Regulation (EU) 2026/131: microchip, rabies vaccination, passport, tapeworm treatment, the UK and Switzerland, and a day-before checklist.">
  <article class="wrap narrow hero prose">
    <div class="travel-head"><div>
      <h1>Travelling with your animal</h1>
      <p class="lead">Since 22 April 2026, holiday and moving trips with dogs, cats and ferrets in the EU follow Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2026/131. It replaced Regulation (EU) No 576/2013. For most owners little changed: chip, rabies vaccination and passport stay, the legal references and forms are new. Here is what every trip needs, what some countries add, and what to check the day before.</p>
    </div><TravelArt /></div>

    <h2>Which law applies</h2>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Regulation in force</th><td>Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2026/131 of 20 January 2026 on non-commercial movement of pet animals, published in the Official Journal on 27 March 2026. Article 33: it applies from 22 April 2026.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>What it replaced</th><td>Regulation (EU) No 576/2013. It was repealed by the Animal Health Law (Regulation (EU) 2016/429) but kept applying to pet travel until 21 April 2026.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Companion acts</th><td>Implementing Regulations (EU) 2026/705 (passport and certificate models) and 2026/636 (non-EU countries whose pets need no rabies blood test).</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Non-commercial means</th><td>The animal travels with you or with someone you authorise in writing, and it is not sold or given to a new owner. If you cannot travel with it, you must travel no more than 5 days before or after it.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What every trip needs</h2>
    <ul class="paw-list">
      <li><strong>Microchip first.</strong> Your animal needs a microchip, and the rabies vaccination must not be dated before the day the chip was implanted or read. A vaccination given before the chip does not count. A clearly readable tattoo applied before 3 July 2011 is still accepted. From 1 January 2028 chips must carry a country code; chips implanted before 22 April 2026 do not need replacing.</li>
      <li><strong>A valid rabies vaccination.</strong> Your animal must be at least 12 weeks old on the day of the first vaccination. After a primary vaccination you wait at least 21 days before the trip. Boosters given before the previous vaccination expires need no new wait.</li>
      <li><strong>Earliest travel age.</strong> 12 weeks plus 21 days means about 15 weeks old. Some countries accept younger, unvaccinated animals; Italy, Germany and Austria do not. Switzerland does, with an owner declaration.</li>
      <li><strong>An EU pet passport.</strong> Issued by a vet authorised by the authorities, in the country where you habitually live. It is only for owners resident in the EU. Passports you already have stay valid; from 1 January 2028 new passports must follow the new model. In Italy the ASL veterinary service issues it, and a dog must be in the dog registry first.</li>
      <li><strong>No more than five animals.</strong> Up to five dogs, cats or ferrets per trip, in one vehicle. More is only allowed for competitions, shows or sporting events (or training for them), with written proof of registration, and every animal must be older than six months. Otherwise the commercial rules apply.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Country-specific extras</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Destination</th><th>What your animal needs on top</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Finland, Ireland, Malta, Northern Ireland</td><td>Dogs only (not cats or ferrets): tapeworm (<em>Echinococcus multilocularis</em>) treatment with praziquantel or an equivalent, given by a vet not more than 120 hours and not less than 24 hours before you enter, recorded in the passport. No treatment needed if you travel directly between these countries. These four are the areas the EU lists as free of the parasite (Implementing Regulation (EU) 2021/620, Annex XIX).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Norway</td><td>Norway is not in the EU and sets this rule itself: dogs need the same tapeworm treatment, 24 to 120 hours before arrival, given by an authorised vet in the country they travel from and recorded in the passport. Not needed for dogs coming directly from Finland, Ireland or Malta.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Great Britain (England, Scotland, Wales), if you live in the EU</td><td>An EU pet passport is accepted. Dogs need the same tapeworm treatment, 24 hours to 5 days (120 hours) before you arrive, unless you come directly from Finland, Ireland, Northern Ireland, Malta or Norway. You must use an approved route, except from Ireland or within the UK.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>EU, if you live in Great Britain</td><td>An EU pet passport is not valid, even one issued in the EU. You need an animal health certificate from a vet for each trip: valid 10 days to enter the EU, then 6 months for onward EU travel and return to Great Britain. Owners living in Northern Ireland can use an EU pet passport.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Into the EU from a non-EU country</td><td>An EU animal health certificate (valid 10 days to entry), a non-commercial declaration, and entry through a travellers' point of entry. From countries not on the 2026/636 list: a rabies blood test showing at least 0.5 IU/ml, taken at least 30 days after vaccination and at least 90 days before the certificate. Young unvaccinated animals cannot enter. Exceptions: from Andorra, the Faroe Islands, Gibraltar, Greenland, Iceland, Liechtenstein, Monaco, San Marino, Switzerland and the Vatican City (Annex I to 2026/636) no travellers' point of entry is needed, and a pet passport issued there can replace the certificate. An animal from the EU coming back from a trip outside the EU travels on its EU pet passport: the rabies vaccination must still be valid and, unless the country is on the 2026/636 lists, the blood test must have been done with a favourable result before leaving (the 90-day wait does not apply). Certificates on the old model issued before 1 October 2026 are accepted until 31 March 2027.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anywhere else</td><td>Check the official page for your destination. National rules on dog breeds, muzzles and leads also apply.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Italy, Germany, Austria and Switzerland</h2>
    <p>Between Italy, Germany and Austria the EU rules apply as above: chip, valid rabies vaccination, EU pet passport. None of the three requires tapeworm treatment for dogs arriving from the others, and none accepts animals too young to be vaccinated. Austria reminds you that municipalities and provinces can add muzzle and lead rules.</p>
    <p>Switzerland is not in the EU, but the EU pet travel rules also apply there. The Swiss Federal Food Safety and Veterinary Office (FSVO/BLV) lists Regulation (EU) 2026/131 among its legal bases. To enter from the EU, your animal needs the same three things: microchip, valid rabies vaccination, recognised pet passport (the Swiss passport counts). The limit of five per private vehicle applies, and dogs with docked ears or tails may not be brought in. Coming back into the EU from Switzerland you need no rabies blood test and no special point of entry. Swiss customs rules still apply at the border.</p>

    <h2>Airlines and trains</h2>
    <p>The law sets the health rules, not the travel conditions. Each airline, railway, ferry and bus company sets its own rules on cabin or hold, carrier sizes, muzzles, tickets and fees, and these change often. Check with your carrier when you book and again a few days before you leave. If your animal flies in the hold, it still counts as travelling with you as long as you are on the same flight.</p>

    <h2 id="lost-passport">If the passport is lost or stolen</h2>
    <p>The EU, German and Italian pages we read (sources below) set out what a passport must contain, but none of them describes a procedure for a lost one. What they do say matters here: the rabies vaccination history in the passport is required, a booster only continues protection if it is given before the previous vaccination runs out, and Germany's ministry says an existing passport stays valid as long as rabies protection is recorded without gaps. If that record cannot be shown, your animal may have to start again with a new primary vaccination and wait 21 days before travelling. Only an authorised vet can decide what can be re-entered.</p>
    <ol class="paw-list">
      <li>Call the practice that gave the last rabies vaccination. Ask for a copy of their record: vaccine name, batch, date given, valid-until date, and the microchip number they read.</li>
      <li>Ask an authorised vet whether a new passport can be issued with that history, or whether a new vaccination is needed. Do this well before any trip.</li>
      <li>If it was stolen, report it to the police and keep the report; some vets or authorities may ask for it.</li>
      <li>Check the microchip registry entry still shows your current phone number.</li>
      <li>From now on, keep a backup: photograph every page of the new passport and keep the <a href="/passport">health booklet</a> up to date, with batch numbers and dates. Its printout is your own copy, not an official document, but it gives the vet everything to check against their records.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>The day before</h2>
    <ul class="paw-list">
      <li>Passport (or animal health certificate) in your hand luggage, original, not a copy. Bring older passports too if they hold a blood test result.</li>
      <li>Rabies vaccination still valid on your return date, not only on the way out.</li>
      <li>Dog going to Finland, Ireland, Malta, Norway or the UK: tapeworm treatment booked so that you arrive between 24 and 120 hours after it. Count in hours, not days, and allow for delays.</li>
      <li>Owner signature in the passport. If someone else travels with the animal, your signed written authorisation attached.</li>
      <li>No more than five animals in the vehicle.</li>
      <li>Carrier rules re-checked; crate or carrier of the right size; lead, muzzle if required.</li>
      <li>Usual food, water, medicines, waste bags; your vet's number and one near your destination.</li>
      <li>Contact details in the registry up to date, so a lost animal can be traced abroad.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the vet checks at the appointment</h2>
    <ul class="paw-list">
      <li>Reads the microchip and checks the number matches the passport.</li>
      <li>Checks the chip date comes before the first rabies vaccination, and that the vaccination is valid for the whole trip; gives a booster if it would expire.</li>
      <li>Fills in or updates the passport, including your details and signature, and tells you if the treatment pages are full and you need a new one.</li>
      <li>For a dog going to a tapeworm-free country: gives the treatment and records the product, date, time, stamp and signature.</li>
      <li>For trips outside the EU: whether you need a rabies blood test before you leave so your animal can come back, and how long it takes.</li>
      <li>A general check: fitness to travel, motion sickness, and parasite prevention for the destination.</li>
    </ul>

